From 6985d7f9cab1527f991591055947d3307db9efdc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 17:52:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] seo: single route manifest, real lastmod, head dedupe that
 holds
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Three defects found while auditing why 22 of 23 sitemap URLs sit in Search
Console as "Discovered - currently not indexed".

1. Three copies of the route list

   generate-sitemap.mjs and prerender.mjs each kept their own hand-maintained
   array, and App.tsx was the real source of truth. They drifted: /lp/shrm-cp
   shipped, was crawled and indexed by Google on Aug 23, was later removed from
   App.tsx, and neither manifest ever knew. It now serves the 200 noindex
   catch-all shell — a soft 404 on an indexed URL, invisible from both scripts.

   scripts/seo-routes.mjs is now the single manifest. scripts/app-routes.mjs
   parses App.tsx, and scripts/check-routes.mjs fails the build when the two
   disagree in either direction (route missing from the manifest, or manifest
   path App.tsx no longer serves). Wired into `npm run build` ahead of tsc.

2. Every sitemap URL stamped with today's date

   lastmod was `new Date()` at build time, so all 23 URLs claimed to change on
   every deploy. Google discounts lastmod it can see is untrue, which costs the
   sitemap the one freshness signal it has. lastmod is now the last commit date
   of the page's own source file, resolved through the App.tsx route->component
   map. Shallow clones (CI checkout defaults to depth 1) collapse every file to
   the tip commit, so that case is detected and lastmod is omitted rather than
   faked.

3. Head dedupe ran in the DOM, so React undid it

   prerender.mjs removed duplicate meta/link tags with page.evaluate() before
   page.content(). React 19 owns the tags it hoists into <head> and re-inserted
   them on its next commit, so the serialized HTML shipped with the duplicates
   anyway. Every live page went out with 3 <title> elements, an empty
   <link rel="canonical"> ahead of the real one, and an empty
   <meta name="robots"> ahead of the real one.

   The canonical is the one that costs us. Google's 2013 post "5 common
   mistakes with rel=canonical" states that when more than one is specified,
   all of them are ignored. That post now carries Google's outdated-content
   banner and the current canonicalization docs do not restate the rule, so it
   is the last explicit word rather than live guidance — but not a coin worth
   flipping on every page of the site.

   scripts/clean-head.mjs now cleans the serialized string instead, after
   nothing can re-inject into it. The intended title and canonical are read
   from the live DOM first and used to verify the result — a route whose head
   does not come out correct throws and fails the build rather than shipping
   quietly. 399 duplicate/empty tags removed across the 23 routes.

Also: homepage footer rebuilt as a grouped link hub. /about and both
/compare/* pages were previously reachable only via /blog, three hops from
root, while Search Console counted 5 internal links for the whole site. The
two highest commercial-intent URLs are now one hop from the homepage with
descriptive anchor text. Homepage internal links: 8 -> 14 of 23 indexable
routes, everything else at two hops via /blog.

PRERENDER_CHROMIUM env var added so a build can point at an already-present
Chromium instead of requiring `npx playwright install`.

Verified: check-routes passes and fails correctly on injected drift; tsc -b
clean; vite build clean; prerender 23/23; all 23 output pages have exactly one
title, one matching canonical, one description, one robots, 9 OG and 5 Twitter
tags; 99 unit tests pass.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
